IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cpp-core`

-- Canada Pension Plan Act and Regulations -- CALENDAR HELPERS (infrastructure).
--
-- No provision of the Act or the Regulations licenses these rules. They exist because the text
-- counts in months and in time limits everywhere, and eight pieces would otherwise each write their
-- own month arithmetic. The provisions that use them are named in NOTES.md ("Naming conventions").
--
-- A month is the DATE of its first day: March 2026 is YMD 2026 3 1.
-- A time limit is a `Time limit` (cpp-core.l4): days, months or years.
--
-- Month arithmetic goes through `add months` and `add years` from daydate, which clamp to the last
-- day of a shorter month (31 August plus 6 months is 28 February). The Interpretation Act, which
-- would say how the Act counts "6 months after", is not among the texts this encoding reads, so the
-- clamp is an assumption, not a ruling (NOTES.md, fork register, F1).
-- Month 0 and month 13 of Date are NOT reliable: Date 1 0 2026 is 1 January 2026, not December 2025.
-- Never build a date from a month number you have subtracted from; use the rules below.

§ `Calendar helpers`

§§ `Months`

-- The first day of the month that contains a date.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the month of a date` d MEANS YMD (DATE_YEAR d) (DATE_MONTH d) 1

-- The first day of the month after the month that contains a date. December rolls into January.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the month following the month of a date` d MEANS Date 1 ((DATE_MONTH d) PLUS 1) (DATE_YEAR d)

-- A running count of months, so that two months can be subtracted: year times 12 plus month.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month number of a date` d MEANS ((DATE_YEAR d) TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH d)

-- How many calendar months lie from the month of the first date to the month of the second.
-- The day of the month is ignored. It is 0 when both dates fall in one month, negative when the
-- second date falls in an earlier month.
GIVEN a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of months from the month of one date to the month of another` a b MEANS
    (`the month number of a date` b) MINUS (`the month number of a date` a)

§§ `Time limits`

-- The date on which a time limit that starts on a date comes to an end ("N days after d",
-- "6 months after d", "one year after d"). Days are counted by adding N to the date.
GIVEN start IS A DATE
      limit IS A `Time limit`
GIVETH A DATE
`the date a time limit ends` start limit MEANS
    CONSIDER limit
    WHEN `a number of days` n THEN start PLUS n
    WHEN `a number of months` n THEN `add months` start n
    WHEN `a number of years` n THEN `add years` start n

-- The date a time limit goes back to from a date ("not later than twelve months before d").
GIVEN end IS A DATE
      limit IS A `Time limit`
GIVETH A DATE
`the date a time limit before a date goes back to` end limit MEANS
    CONSIDER limit
    WHEN `a number of days` n THEN end PLUS (0 MINUS n)
    WHEN `a number of months` n THEN `add months` end (0 MINUS n)
    WHEN `a number of years` n THEN `add years` end (0 MINUS n)

-- Whether a date falls on or after the start and on or before the end of the time limit that runs from it.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      start IS A DATE
      limit IS A `Time limit`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a date is within a time limit after a start date` d start limit MEANS
    d AT LEAST start AND d AT MOST (`the date a time limit ends` start limit)
